.TH ATLAS-PYTHON-ONELINERS 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-python\-oneliners \- Use \fBpython3 \-c\fR for the small decode, convert and brute\-force jobs that come up in every CTF.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
python3  \-c  "import base64; print(base64.b64decode('aGk=').decode())"
   |      |        |
   |      |        '\-\-\- a program in one string: statements separated by ;
   |      '\-\-\- \-c = run the string as code (use \-m for a module, \- to read a script from stdin)
   '\-\-\- the interpreter
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Decode or encode base64, hex, URL or binary text.
.IP \(bu 2
Convert between number bases and byte orders.
.IP \(bu 2
XOR, hash, brute\-force a short secret, or work out tiny RSA.
.IP \(bu 2
Parse a log with Counter instead of building a long shell pipeline.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-c 'code'\fR
Run code given on the command line.
.br
Use it when: Quick transforms.
.TP
\fB\-m http.server 8000\fR
Run a module: a quick web server for transfers.
.br
Use it when: Serve files in a lab.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR
Stay interactive afterwards.
.br
Use it when: Explore the data you loaded.
.TP
\fB\-\fR
Read the script from stdin.
.br
Use it when: Here\-documents with \fB<<'PY'\fR.
.TP
\fB\-I\fR
Isolated mode: ignore the current folder and user site.
.br
Use it when: Safe use inside an untrusted folder.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR
Unbuffered output.
.br
Use it when: Network scripts and pipes.
.TP
\fBbytes.fromhex(s) / b.hex()\fR
Hex string to bytes and back.
.br
Use it when: Decode hex.
.TP
\fBint(x, base) / bin() / hex() / oct()\fR
Convert between bases.
.br
Use it when: Binary, hex, octal puzzles.
.TP
\fBstruct.pack('<I', n)\fR
Pack a number as little\-endian bytes (\fB>\fR = big\-endian).
.br
Use it when: Build payloads; read binary fields.
.TP
\fBpow(a, \-1, m)\fR
Modular inverse.
.br
Use it when: RSA private exponent.
.TP
\fBitertools.product\fR
Every combination of a character set.
.br
Use it when: Short brute force.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. Decode base64"
Base64 turns bytes into text with \fBA\-Z a\-z 0\-9 + /\fR and \fB=\fR padding. \fBb64decode\fR returns bytes; \fB.decode()\fR makes text.
.PP
.nf
$ python3 \-c "import base64; print(base64.b64decode('aGVsbG8gd29ybGQ=').decode())"
.fi
.PP
.nf
hello world
.fi
.PP
You get \fBhello world\fR. If decoding fails, check for missing \fB=\fR padding, newlines or URL\-safe \fB\-_\fR characters.
.SS "2. Hex and bytes"
Two directions: hex text to bytes, and text to hex.
.PP
.nf
$ python3 \-c "print(bytes.fromhex('666c61677b6865787d')); print('flag'.encode().hex())"
.fi
.PP
.nf
b'flag{hex}'
666c6167
.fi
.PP
The first line is a \fBbytes\fR object (note the \fBb'...'\fR), the second the hex of the word \fBflag\fR.
.SS "3. XOR with a repeating key"
\fBi % len(k)\fR repeats the key. XOR is its own inverse, so the same code encrypts and decrypts.
.PP
.nf
$ python3 \-c "d=bytes.fromhex('0b0d080c'); k=b'AB'; print(bytes(b ^ k[i % len(k)] for i, b in enumerate(d)))"
.fi
.PP
.nf
b'JOIN'
.fi
.PP
The result is readable text. In Natas 11 you recover the key the same way: key = ciphertext XOR known plaintext.
.SS "4. Number bases"
\fBint(text, base)\fR parses; \fBbin\fR, \fBoct\fR, \fBhex\fR format.
.PP
.nf
$ python3 \-c "n=0xdeadbeef; print(n); print(bin(10), oct(64), hex(255)); print(int('777', 8), int('1010', 2), int('ff', 16))"
.fi
.PP
.nf
3735928559
0b1010 0o100 0xff
511 10 255
.fi
.PP
Decimal for the hex value, then conversions both ways. Handy for the picoCTF 'convertme' style tasks.
.SS "5. Byte order (endianness)"
\fB<\fR = little\-endian (x86), \fB>\fR = big\-endian (network). \fBI\fR = 4\-byte unsigned.
.PP
.nf
$ python3 \-c "import struct; print(struct.pack('<I', 0xdeadbeef).hex(), struct.pack('>I', 0xdeadbeef).hex()); print(hex(struct.unpack('<I', bytes.fromhex('efbeadde'))[0]))"
.fi
.PP
.nf
efbeadde deadbeef
0xdeadbeef
.fi
.PP
The same number gives two byte orders. This is the \fBef be ad de\fR you typed in Narnia 0.
.SS "6. Brute\-force a short secret from its hash"
Only 10,000 four\-digit PINs exist, so trying them all is instant. Cost grows as alphabet^length.
.PP
.nf
$ python3 \-c "import hashlib, itertools; t = hashlib.md5(b'4821').hexdigest(); print(t); print(next(''.join(c) for c in itertools.product('0123456789', repeat=4) if hashlib.md5(''.join(c).encode()).hexdigest() == t))"
.fi
.PP
.nf
85267d349a5e647ff0a9edcb5ffd1e02
4821
.fi
.PP
It prints the hash and then recovers \fB4821\fR. Real password cracking uses hashcat or john, but the idea is the same.
.SS "7. Count things in a log"
\fBCounter\fR is awk's associative array with sorting built in.
.PP
.nf
$ python3 \-c "import collections; print(collections.Counter(l.split()[0] for l in open('access.log')).most_common(2))"
.fi
.PP
.nf
[('10.0.0.9', 5), ('10.0.0.5', 2)]
.fi
.PP
The two busiest IPs with their counts.
.SS "8. Tiny RSA"
With the factors p and q you can compute the private exponent d. \fBpow(c, d, n)\fR decrypts.
.PP
.nf
$ python3 \-c "p, q, e = 61, 53, 17; n = p * q; d = pow(e, \-1, (p \- 1) * (q \- 1)); m = 65; c = pow(m, e, n); print(n, d, c, pow(c, d, n))"
.fi
.PP
.nf
3233 2753 2790 65
.fi
.PP
\fBn=3233\fR, \fBd=2753\fR, a ciphertext, and the original message 65 again. CTF RSA tasks give you weak numbers where you must find p and q.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
Quote carefully: wrap the program in double quotes in bash and use single quotes inside (or the other way round).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprint(bytes)\fR shows \fBb'...'\fR: call \fB.decode()\fR for text.
.IP \(bu 2
Python 2 is gone: \fBprint\fR needs parentheses.
.IP \(bu 2
Integer division is \fB//\fR; \fB/\fR gives a float, which breaks big\-number maths.
.IP \(bu 2
For anything longer than three lines, write a file and add comments.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Decode a base64 string, then hex\-decode the result.
.IP 2. 3
XOR two hex strings of equal length.
.IP 3. 3
Find the PIN whose sha1 equals a hash of your choice.
.SH SEE ALSO
CyberChef, xxd, base64, pwntools, pycryptodome, sympy, z3.
